# Who has to approve what.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for "which files are frozen." The
# frozen-files table in CLAUDE.md and the guard job in
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` both describe this list — the guard job reads it
# from here at runtime, so add a path once and it takes effect everywhere.
#
# How it works: the branch ruleset on `main` has "Require review from Code
# Owners" on, and GitHub only requests a code owner's review when a pull
# request actually changes a file matching one of the patterns below. So:
#
#   src/content/site.ts   → matches nothing → no approval needed, Alysa merges
#   wrangler.jsonc        → matches → McKay's review requested, merge blocked
#
# That is deliberate. Alysa can iterate on copy and design at her own pace,
# clicking through Cloudflare previews and merging when CI is green, and McKay
# is only pulled in when a change touches something that could take the site
# down or weaken a guardrail.
#
# The gate keys off *paths*, not the `infra` label. A label is self-declared —
# it can be forgotten or removed. Editing `wrangler.jsonc` always trips this
# file. The label still exists, and CI enforces that it is present, but it is
# for triage, not for security.
#
# NOTE: there is deliberately no `*` line. Adding one would put McKay back on
# every pull request and undo all of the above.

# CI, the reviewer requirement itself, and Dependabot's config.
/.github/ @mckayqsnell

# The rules Claude works under.
/CLAUDE.md @mckayqsnell

# What the site is built out of. Covers Dependabot's version-bump PRs, which
# is intended — a dependency change should get a human look.
/package.json @mckayqsnell
/pnpm-lock.yaml @mckayqsnell
/pnpm-workspace.yaml @mckayqsnell

# How the site gets built and deployed.
/wrangler.jsonc @mckayqsnell
/vite.config.ts @mckayqsnell
/tsconfig*.json @mckayqsnell

# How the checks that gate every merge are configured.
/eslint.config.js @mckayqsnell
/playwright.config.ts @mckayqsnell
